 "notes": "Unit is excavation row E334 (Tell es-Sultan); its own components list no pool, pit or grave. Rows with only natural niches, recesses, fissures, cliffs or rock shelters, and rows marked 'not examined, inaccessible' (S2361, S2356) or with empty components (E346, E322) were skipped; E325 has empty components but is named 'Cave No. 20', so recorded as a cave. Caves, cavities and cave passages coded as pit. 'Human bones'/'skeletons' in refuge caves (S2341, S2342, S2339, E332, E333, E331, S2350, S2233) not coded as graves because no source calls them burials. Duplicate S/E rows of the same site each coded. SWP: entry 0 is a cross-reference only. In the Tell es Sultan entry, Warren's 'shafts' are excavation trenches, not features; mound No. 3 graves are 'south of 'Ain es Sultan' (the spring), so relative_to other. Entries 2-9 were found because their text names the unit; they describe other places not shown to be within 2 km and were ignored: Kusr el Yehud (monastery of St John near the Jordan, cistern), Rujm el Mogheifir (cisterns; south beyond Wady Kelt at the end of an aqueduct), Beit Jubr et Tahtani (birkeh; matched only via 'Birket es Sultan at Jerusalem'), Kanat Musa (regional aqueduct system; cisterns at other places), Hajr el Asbah (6 miles SW of 'Ain Hajlah), Khurbet es Sumrah (caves; square Pr), Kanat el Manil (aqueduct from 'Ain el 'Aujeh), Khurbet el Mefjir (no pool, pit or grave). OCR of the SWP text is noisy but readable. Nigro: N85 is the unit's own site (2 m, bearing invalid) and its features take grid N85. N21 Roman 'nympheum' and N96 'ritual baths' coded pool with pool_def unclear; N21 modern 'roofed pool' coded pit. N47 has no coordinates: cisterns placed 'south-west of site n. 46' (relative to N46). N75 'water supply system' and 'moat' not coded (no cistern or pool named). N49 has no feature."
}